2a.py	Clean, well-structured solution that correctly enforces letter-only words over 10 characters and uses random sampling of unique words; great job breaking logic into functions and handling common file errors.
i9.py	Strong beginner program with clear helper functions and good exception handling; using a set for uniqueness and random.sample for output is correct, and this is very close to an ideal intro-level submission.
y6.py	Nice concise approach that lowercases, strips punctuation, filters correctly, and samples unique words, but adding argument/file error handling and guarding against fewer than 10 matches would make it more robust.
y7.py	Good effort with comments and exception handling, and the random unique output works, but not lowercasing can cause duplicate words by case and the extra informational prints go beyond the assignment output.
nt.py	Good use of set and random.sample with the right length check, but not removing punctuation means many valid long words are skipped when attached to commas/periods, so results may be incomplete.
35.py	You successfully build a unique list and sample random words, but missing punctuation cleanup and file/argument error handling makes this less reliable for real text files like the test input.
sc.py	You did a solid job reading and normalizing text, but this version misses key requirements because it does not filter for words over 10 letters and does not actually pick random words (set.pop is not true random selection).